[{:db/id "q-21044/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" :answer/id #uuid "3e5656ac-ac8f-5055-b399-4151121efff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21044/selection/4507af753e94c6f82db929d331e6749b5de730f7a1cfa515ef7e9eae88a0fbc8" :answer/id #uuid "414ea75b-8727-5abf-83c0-7d6fa0792fcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21044/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b" :answer/id #uuid "e8041d77-d7eb-58cc-8ea9-6063c5e44c3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21044/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" :answer/id #uuid "bf1f1ddb-0270-5e1d-adff-a3b8d9343b08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21044/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer/id #uuid "53d9943a-8225-5215-ae4f-3a22c803bbb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21044/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e99b56f-26de-5c42-a6c2-058da69e7299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21044/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" :answer-field/choices ["q-21044/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" "q-21044/selection/4507af753e94c6f82db929d331e6749b5de730f7a1cfa515ef7e9eae88a0fbc8" "q-21044/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b" "q-21044/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" "q-21044/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21044" :question/id #uuid "f01f8756-8970-5015-bf48-1f08a36f1f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-21044" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=6{x}^{3}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-21044/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21044"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045/selection/9626c66eef2f3f9240fcaefd6592dfbf4c7505bfc6b028b9e6a85a927af5ca55" :answer/id #uuid "d1ef19e4-9c76-5d48-af86-c3384933995e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" :answer/id #uuid "d400d042-6b8d-5919-88e8-7968b6797b48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045/selection/2f080de0ef65f6e5dd2806fa211066253fb2f37c1d406be458ab4efcf0eceed2" :answer/id #uuid "a7789eb0-c545-500f-9db4-c62c1aae5fc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045/selection/264ed6922279f92bdc90c36a26ea47de8f6c6ab903ed9e63c8b8bb51a5a9d4f6" :answer/id #uuid "0a48152b-7428-5af0-b317-2a40ba1fca1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" :answer/id #uuid "d249f77e-2330-5878-bf0f-eed7a9569b47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "25f144ac-f989-5ca1-a069-599509696ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21045/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-21045/selection/9626c66eef2f3f9240fcaefd6592dfbf4c7505bfc6b028b9e6a85a927af5ca55" "q-21045/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" "q-21045/selection/2f080de0ef65f6e5dd2806fa211066253fb2f37c1d406be458ab4efcf0eceed2" "q-21045/selection/264ed6922279f92bdc90c36a26ea47de8f6c6ab903ed9e63c8b8bb51a5a9d4f6" "q-21045/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21045" :question/id #uuid "17c2a431-b9a6-5e59-b365-e7f1bd9e8f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-21045" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-3x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.  \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=2$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-2=-2<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =2(2)-2=2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-21045/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21045"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203/selection/b50601e273d79e10e9e358058a47fa86921697a94fe57c321b8a57c239166502" :answer/id #uuid "012d96b4-f5c4-5fdd-ad9b-15a24af95082" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer/id #uuid "6ae5b1bb-5e50-5eb2-9662-afa9cb07a12b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203/selection/937b41f56da576bd9796cd489168795e3d004c4b393af5f374f5a350b7acafbd" :answer/id #uuid "f73b1650-e5a9-54ac-bfeb-d76db311d3c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" :answer/id #uuid "958f54a6-95cb-5192-8a3a-e12c13b3e6e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a" :answer/id #uuid "c07530c9-601d-5133-90e6-696dc3e202bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3ae0959-70ec-5fca-85ba-b0492538a831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79203/selection/937b41f56da576bd9796cd489168795e3d004c4b393af5f374f5a350b7acafbd" :answer-field/choices ["q-79203/selection/b50601e273d79e10e9e358058a47fa86921697a94fe57c321b8a57c239166502" "q-79203/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" "q-79203/selection/937b41f56da576bd9796cd489168795e3d004c4b393af5f374f5a350b7acafbd" "q-79203/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" "q-79203/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79203" :question/id #uuid "c7d0fd56-6605-5190-9333-e6bf3c07cfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79203" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/2c0a892cd217758e2871f2d3f6f18d7615e79e0f5ad792c751c662e35c69e168.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79203/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79203"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "c6638664-b046-596c-9880-f021d212a6f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" :answer/id #uuid "bc6936e8-d65a-51c6-a47e-c9913e49b7f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419/selection/b014d7e43bc1c71454284a7643cb2c82c70ba21a301c02d2667b5890761beac4" :answer/id #uuid "645c3376-2379-5b97-9d16-5b2e31efb73b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419/selection/565f087efc172cc689d6ac7a38ae1818e245a6f8995ecba68bcdf0ac5950b2ea" :answer/id #uuid "8ab2dd6b-9c79-5fe4-9925-637166131c3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419/selection/017ec74e48021b90d41305ba8cdc32b5df2a5e0d6620fb190ddde80f6dd00a99" :answer/id #uuid "addff524-8fb9-5760-a710-1eaf0806f2cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4caa3e0-51b9-5fe0-9280-4fa651b69b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114419/selection/b014d7e43bc1c71454284a7643cb2c82c70ba21a301c02d2667b5890761beac4" :answer-field/choices ["q-114419/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" "q-114419/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" "q-114419/selection/b014d7e43bc1c71454284a7643cb2c82c70ba21a301c02d2667b5890761beac4" "q-114419/selection/565f087efc172cc689d6ac7a38ae1818e245a6f8995ecba68bcdf0ac5950b2ea" "q-114419/selection/017ec74e48021b90d41305ba8cdc32b5df2a5e0d6620fb190ddde80f6dd00a99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114419" :question/id #uuid "2e1a1dd3-3388-5f3d-8d7a-a3ff07dd42a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/036e249b188b46fe94bd6955fce114cb11dc0f1e1c169fb58d36135cfbc9b797.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has no inflection points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114419/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114419"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
